    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 960,
      "versionNonce": 1170852786,
      "index": "c03v",
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "NJ8pJoXM4HqtZiFDBY4zz",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": -209.76071021670464,
      "y": 6670.00773809523,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 1073.4000244140625,
      "height": 1260,
      "seed": 978716270,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790189642653,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "text": "--> What happens when we deal with \n    ° groups :\n\n    ==> What you need to do is :\n        ° a group synchronization\n\n--> Imagine for a moment that you've got \n    a group called :\n        ° openshift-admins\n\n--> And of course, the group membership \n    is subject to change :\n        ° So, you have someone that has left\n     and \n        ° someone new that is joining the team. \n     \n    ==> What you need to do is go to your LDAP provider :\n         ° Remove the user that has left the company\n         ° Remove them from the openshift-admins group\n         ° And then take the new hire and make them a\n           member of the openshift-admins group\n\n--> Now you don't want to do a manual synchronization\n    of the groups from the LDAP provider to the\n    openshift cluster\n    ==> we want to take advantage of automation\n    ==> we would use, to do the group synchronization, \n        we would implement that as a cron job",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "--> What happens when we deal with \n    ° groups :\n\n    ==> What you need to do is :\n        ° a group synchronization\n\n--> Imagine for a moment that you've got \n    a group called :\n        ° openshift-admins\n\n--> And of course, the group membership \n    is subject to change :\n        ° So, you have someone that has left\n     and \n        ° someone new that is joining the team. \n     \n    ==> What you need to do is go to your LDAP provider :\n         ° Remove the user that has left the company\n         ° Remove them from the openshift-admins group\n         ° And then take the new hire and make them a\n           member of the openshift-admins group\n\n--> Now you don't want to do a manual synchronization\n    of the groups from the LDAP provider to the\n    openshift cluster\n    ==> we want to take advantage of automation\n    ==> we would use, to do the group synchronization, \n        we would implement that as a cron job",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790189642647,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
